{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And the man took a golden ring, a beka in weight, and two bracelets.</b> Why was it necessary to inform us of the weight? Rather, it is a hint to you through these gifts that from her there would emerge a nation of receivers of the Torah, who would bring shekels, concerning which it is written 'a beka per head' (בקע לגולגלת), and who would receive the two tablets of the covenant, which are the Ten Commandments, and they contain 172 words — and this is 'and two bracelets upon her hands, ten gold their weight.' Furthermore, the word שקל is a notarikon of אש קול ('fire, voice'), hinting at the giving of the Torah, concerning which it is written there (Deuteronomy 4:36): 'From heaven He made you hear His voice to discipline you, and on earth He showed you His great fire.'" "<b>And from this</b> you should reflect on the matter of Haman the wicked, who said: 'and ten thousand talents of silver I will weigh' (ועשרת אלפים ככר כסף אשקול), through the doers of the work, for his evil intention was to destroy the enemies of Israel and to nullify with his 'ten' the 'ten' that were given through the fire and the voice. And this is what is hinted here in the prayer 'I, on the road, guide me, HaShem' (אנכי בדרך נחני ה'), for the merit of the Torah, which is 'I' (אנכי), whose children were destined to receive it, was with him in the success of the journey." "<b>And behold,</b> all these events that happened to Rebekah were all a sign for her children. And the events that happened to the servant in the success of the journey parallel the events that happened to her children in the way of the wilderness. Just as an angel was with him on the journey by virtue of Abraham's prayer, who said, 'He will send His angel before you' (הוא ישלח מלאכו לפניך), so we find her children in the way of the wilderness (Exodus 23:20): 'Behold, I send an angel before you' (הנה אנכי שולח מלאך לפניך). And because it was the particular angel among the distinct angels, therefore it said מלאכו — that is, the one particular to him, and he is the one prince emanated from the attribute of Abraham. And this is (ibid.) 'for My angel shall go' (כי ילך מלאכי); Abraham called him here מלאכו, and the Holy One, blessed be He, called him מלאכי. And just as the waters rose to meet her, so with her children in the way of the wilderness (Numbers 21:17): 'Rise up, O well, sing to it' (עלי באר ענו לה). And the servant who gave her these gifts hinted to her through them: for just as she received these gifts through a servant, so her children were destined to receive the Torah through Moses, the servant of HaShem, who was the faithful servant in whose hand was all the goodness of his master, as it is written (Exodus 33:19): 'I will cause all My goodness to pass' (אני אעביר כל טובי). And just as he gave her many gifts, some on the journey and some in the house — and those on the journey were 'a golden ring, a beka in weight, and two bracelets, ten gold their weight' — so her children in the wilderness brought shekels and received the two tablets of the covenant, in which were the Ten Commandments. And just as he gave her gifts in the house as well, besides those he gave her on the journey, as it is written 'And the servant brought out vessels of silver and vessels of gold,' so her children in the land of Moab, close to their entry into the land, were given many commandments, as it is written (Deuteronomy 28:69): 'These are the words of the covenant which HaShem commanded Moses to cut with the children of Israel in the land of Moab, besides the covenant which He cut with them at Horeb.'" "<b>And from this</b> — the Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'The Torah reveals and makes known; it was given.' And just as this passage was doubled in the matter of the servant, twice, and the servant went back to recount all the things that happened to him on the journey, so her children received the Torah in first tablets and second tablets, and Moses, the servant of HaShem, in Deuteronomy went back to recount beyond the Jordan all the things that had happened to them concerning the first tablets. And these matters that were hinted at in the marriage of Isaac and Rebekah, who bore Jacob and his sons, from whom emerged the nation of receivers of the Torah — all this was hinted at in the sending of the servant according to the degree of the Patriarchs."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:55.124556954Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}